A||||11||KnightRyder31||Bad Noise from engine(2.5)||||1||
Z||000000||KnightRyder31||11-03-2000||05:24 PM||||I'm hearing a ticking noise. Getting worse day by day. Some people tell me its a gasket, some say lifters, some say exhaust man. What the hell? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||11-03-2000||05:59 PM||||If its getting worse everyday, I'd guess exhaust...manifold is probably cracked. Happens even to the 2.5.<P>And the principles of thermodynamics dictate that an exhaust leak would tend to be louder as the weather got cooler...<P>Disclaimer...I don't know anything about the laws of thermodynamics.<P>Try using a length of heater hose to listen for the problem. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||KnightRyder31||11-04-2000||09:58 AM||||Is there any way to be sure without taking it to a specialist? How hard would it be to fix? Will a cracked exhaust man. take anything away from my car, except the fact that it makes this crapy noise?<p>[This message has been edited by KnightRyder31 (edited 11-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-04-2000||09:19 PM||||A cracked exhaust can be quite dangerous. depends on the crack. If it's in the manifold, which wouldn't suprise me, then it will also throw off the O2 sensor readings. It might not set a code but it will affect performance and fuel economy.<P>Do the hose to the ear thing and listen around like deceler8 said. That should give you a starting point.<P>A cracked manifold wouldn't suprise me because I'm on my third one since I got the car. Water spills on it when you open the trunk lid and the crash cooling isn't good for it. Also, If the dogbone is bad you can break the exhaust parts with the excess vibration and engine movement.||||reg||1
Z||000004||KnightRyder31||11-05-2000||12:47 PM||||So how can I be sure? <BR>I really don't have the money to take it to someone.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||11-05-2000||01:40 PM||||Take about a four foot length of garden hose or heater hose. Put one end against your ear. Move the other end all around the exhaust manifold, with the engine running. (Watch out for the belts and pulleys!) When you get close to the leak, you will hear it very clearly. Don't touch the hot manifold with your hand or the hose. Your hand will burn, the hose will melt. Another place to check would be the coupling, where the exhaust system connects to the headpipe. (It's the big coupling, just in front of the transmission.)<P>Raydar||||reg||1
Z||000006||KnightRyder31||11-06-2000||06:19 PM||||Now someone from the Michigan Fiero Club says its either a cracked piston, or a lifter. He said its one of the two, for sure! NO!!!!<BR>Please tell me he's wrong!!!! <BR>PLEASE!!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||KnightRyder31||11-11-2000||10:05 AM||||I also notice that when I accellerate, I hear a lot of wierd noises. Like sheet metel hitting together. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Indiana_resto_guy||11-11-2000||11:02 AM||||Check all your heat shields as well. They tend to vibrate loose with age and corrode around mounting points. Check them all and tighten or add washers if loose.||||reg||1
Z||000009||KnightRyder31||11-11-2000||10:45 PM||||Heat Shields???||||reg||1
Z||000010||Indiana_resto_guy||11-12-2000||01:20 PM||||Heat shields:<P>Large metal plate above convertor.<BR>Large metal plate in front of trunk area.<BR>There may also be one on the exhaust side of the engine.<P>You may also check the inspection cover between engine and transmission and make sure that it is secure.<P>These can rattle, vibrate, make ticking noises that are generally worse during acceleration and clutch release but tend to get quieter at steady RPMs.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Indiana_resto_guy||11-12-2000||01:20 PM||||Heat shields:<BR>OOPS!<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 11-12-2000).]||||reg||1
